course: dictionary
lesson: 13587
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one to use. The word inclusive has three. «Read pages 5 to 10 inclusive for homework.» Here inclusive means the first and last numbers count too, so you read pages 5, 6, 7, 8, 9 and 10.
explain: «Our new club is inclusive, so everyone can join in.» Here inclusive means welcoming everyone and leaving nobody out. Ask yourself: which meaning fits the sentence? Then swap your meaning in and check that it still makes sense.
explain: «The holiday price is inclusive of food.» Inclusive of means the food is already counted in the price. You do not pay extra for it. The words around inclusive, such as price and food, are the clues that show you which meaning the writer chose.
fact: Inclusive means including the first and last item named: «pages 5 to 10 inclusive» means 5, 6, 7, 8, 9 and 10.
fact: Inclusive means welcoming everyone and leaving nobody out.
fact: Inclusive of means including something in the price or total, as in «inclusive of tax».
fact: In maths, «from 3 to 7 inclusive» means 5 numbers, because both ends are counted.
fact: «All-inclusive» means everything is included in the price, and «an inclusive school» is a school where everyone belongs.
exercise: «Read pages 5 to 10 inclusive for tomorrow.» How many pages must you read? | 10 | 5 | *6 | 4
exercise: «Our school is inclusive, so every child can join the games.» What does inclusive mean? | Only for the fastest runners | Closed on Fridays | Shiny and new | *Welcoming everyone and leaving nobody out
exercise: «The ticket price is inclusive of lunch.» What does inclusive of mean here? | Lunch costs extra | Lunch is cold | *Lunch is included in the price | Lunch is not allowed
test: «From 3 to 7 inclusive, how many numbers are there?» | *5 | 6 | 4 | 7
test: Which sentence shows an inclusive club? | *The club welcomes everyone, even new children | The club closes at four | The club is only for the fastest swimmers | The club sells cakes
exam: Sam says «all-inclusive» means some things are missing from the price. Is Sam right? | *No, all-inclusive means everything is included | Yes, only the drinks are included | Yes, some things are always left out | No, it means the price is cut in half
